1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,377
Most visits to Qatar's primary health centres are walk-ins.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,698
Each one starts at the front desk, where staff record why
you came.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,762
Then come triage, the doctor and the pharmacy.

4
00:00:14,912 --> 00:00:19,394
Qatar's apps, Nar-aakom and Lbaih, handle appointments and
records.

5
00:00:19,544 --> 00:00:23,100
But in our research, we found no digital path for walk-ins.

6
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:24,123
That is the gap.

7
00:00:24,273 --> 00:00:26,519
This is Dawri, دوري, which means 'my turn'.

8
00:00:26,669 --> 00:00:28,551
Dawri is an AI front desk.

9
00:00:28,701 --> 00:00:35,967
Fatima, 34, checks in on her phone before she arrives. This
is a demo, with synthetic patients.

10
00:00:36,117 --> 00:00:42,172
She tells the AI receptionist, in Arabic, by text or voice:
fever and cough for three days.

11
00:00:42,322 --> 00:00:44,196
It asks a few short questions,

12
00:00:44,346 --> 00:00:45,283
one at a time.

13
00:00:45,433 --> 00:00:48,004
She picks a centre on a map with estimated waits.

14
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,561
That is a reservation, not yet a place in line.

15
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:06,088
When she arrives, she taps 'I'm here'. Her ticket activates
and gets a number, T-014. Order comes from arrival and the
centre's own priority rule. Plain code, no AI.

16
00:01:06,238 --> 00:01:11,498
At triage, the nurse sees her exact words, the AI's intake,
and her penicillin allergy.

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,179
The nurse enters vitals and confirms the handoff to the
doctor.

18
00:01:15,329 --> 00:01:20,428
The doctor prescribes, and the system warns about
amoxicillin, because of her penicillin allergy.

19
00:01:20,578 --> 00:01:22,817
Then the pharmacy, and the visit is done.

20
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,889
Ahmed asks for his usual blood-pressure pills. He is offered
the refill route, with no ticket.

21
00:01:30,039 --> 00:01:32,976
Ravi types that he has chest pain spreading to his arm.

22
00:01:33,126 --> 00:01:39,590
A rule catches it first. He sees 'call 999' and the nearest
hospital, and gets no ticket.

23
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,315
Safety does not depend on the model behaving.

24
00:01:44,465 --> 00:01:52,431
Rules check every message for emergency wording, in Arabic
and English, and a hit stops the chat. The model cannot
overrule it.

25
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,824
A model's reply reaches the patient only if it is one short,
plain question. The AI never diagnoses and never prescribes.

26
00:02:00,974 --> 00:02:06,239
A nurse confirms everything before the doctor sees it, and
the queue is code the AI cannot touch.

27
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,014
And we say plainly: no clinician has reviewed our red-flag
list yet.

28
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,657
Under the hood: a FastAPI service with SQLite and a React
app, in one Docker container on a Hugging Face Space.

29
00:02:20,807 --> 00:02:30,880
Chat, intake and the nurse's handoff draft run on Gemma 4
26B, through Hugging Face Inference Providers. Whisper
large-v3-turbo handles speech.

30
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:38,817
We chose Gemma after testing sixteen model and provider
pairs. We wanted Qatar's Fanar, but it had no provider
capacity.

31
00:02:38,967 --> 00:02:43,525
Our fine-tuned Fanar 9B lost emergency recognition, so we
kept it out.

32
00:02:43,675 --> 00:02:50,678
Our aim: take the reception step off the walk-in's path, and
give nurses a structured intake, with people deciding
everything clinical.

33
00:02:50,828 --> 00:02:57,391
This is a prototype. Going live would need partnership, a
clinical review and data-protection approval.

34
00:02:57,541 --> 00:02:58,055
Dawri.

35
00:02:58,205 --> 00:02:59,760
Arrive, don't register.
